# <%= current_page.data.title %>

The GOV.UK Content API is a public, read-only API for retrieving content and metadata
from supported pages on the [GOV.UK][gov-uk] website. This information is available
to any member of the public, but the API provides it in a format that your services,
tools and products can consume directly.

This technical documentation gives you information on:

- how the GOV.UK Content API works
- what you can and cannot use the API for
- how to retrieve and display GOV.UK content

[Contact us][support] if you have any questions or feedback.

## What you can do with this API

You can use the GOV.UK Content API for building services, tools and products which
incorporate content from GOV.UK, and for keeping that content up to date.

The GOV.UK Content API returns a [JSON][json-def] representation of a page from
the [GOV.UK][gov-uk] website. The response includes page metadata, links to
related content, and the page body as HTML. This provides a more convenient and
predictable interface than scraping the HTML from web pages.

The information returned by the API includes links between related GOV.UK content.
For example, a response may identify the government organisation that published
a page. You could then retrieve that organisation's policies from another page.

Our [API reference][api-reference] describes the API endpoints and response format
a developer can expect.

## What you can't do with this API

The content and metadata you can access using the GOV.UK Content API are limited
to pages on the [GOV.UK][gov-uk] website, published at the `www.gov.uk` domain.
For example, here are some pages that you **can** access using the API:

- ["Holiday entitlement rights" on `www.gov.uk`][holiday-entitlement-rights]
- ["Registering for Self Assessment" on `www.gov.uk`][self-assessment-tax-returns]
- ["Pay and conditions" on `www.gov.uk`][pay-and-conditions]

The GOV.UK Content API does not provide access to content from websites on other
subdomains of `gov.uk`, such as `blog.gov.uk`. The following are examples of pages
that you **cannot** access using the API:

- ["Transforming towns and cities for people and nature" on `blog.gov.uk`][people-and-nature]
- ["Using GOV.UK Pay" on `payments.service.gov.uk`][using-govuk-pay]

### Types of content available from this API

Some fields in the API response from the GOV.UK Content API contain HTML. This
HTML may reference images, attachments or other files, but you cannot use the API
to return those files directly. Instead you must retrieve, for example, an image
file directly from its source by other means.

In addition, you can only access content from static pages on the GOV.UK website
using this API. Content from dynamic pages (for example, from a search, or using
data that changes over time) is not available from this API.

Some types of content are still being migrated to the GOV.UK Content API. In such
cases, you may encounter a placeholder page which holds no content, or a redirect
to a catch-all destination for unmigrated content. You can find more information
in the [API reference][api-reference].

You cannot access historic content using this API. If you want to access historic
content, you can get this from the [UK Government Web Archive][national-archives].

## Who can use this API

The GOV.UK Content API is public, and anyone can use it. You do not need to seek
permission or sign any agreements to use this API.

